title: IAM policy languages and Cedar, hands-on
guide: howto/iam-policy-languages.html
promise: Read AWS IAM policies in AWS's evaluation order, learn the elements that invert, then write, validate and test Cedar policies.

== Who it is for and what you need
section: How to use this guide
It is for intermediate readers who know IAM (Identity and Access Management) users, roles and policies and have run the AWS CLI (command-line interface). Cedar is taught from the start.

You need a Rust toolchain and AWS CLI credentials.

== How the guide was checked
section: How to use this guide
A script ran every code block on 4 November 2026 with AWS CLI 2.39.2, against an account outside any organization, and cedar-policy-cli 4.14.1.

Not run: attaching a resource control policy, which needs an organization. AWS's documentation says what one does.

== How to follow the guide
section: How to use this guide
JSON blocks are policy text: save one when the text gives a file name. Save CEDAR blocks in ~/cedar-lab, named by their first line. Paste BASH blocks into a terminal in the folder holding their files. Unlabelled blocks are output.

== Set up your environment
section: 8. Install it and write a policy set
AWS CLI commands need credentials for any AWS account, no organization needed, and create nothing. The Cedar steps, 8 to 10, need a Rust toolchain and no AWS account. Step 8 installs cedar-policy-cli; compare cedar --version with the guide's 4.14.1.
```bash
cargo install cedar-policy-cli
cedar --version

mkdir -p ~/cedar-lab && cd ~/cedar-lab
```

== Two policy languages and where you meet them
section: Reading a policy correctly is its own skill
An IAM policy is a JSON document saying who may do what to which AWS resources. Almost everyone in cloud security reads them.

Cedar is AWS's open-source language for deciding whether requests inside your own applications are allowed.

== Policies that grant, and guardrails that cap
section: How AWS decides whether a request is allowed
Identity policies, on the requesting user or role, and resource policies, on the thing accessed, grant access. Guardrails only cap it; they include a role's permissions boundary and an organization's service control policies (SCPs) and resource control policies (RCPs).

== The one idea: Allow may grant nothing
section: How AWS decides whether a request is allowed
AWS combines every policy that applies, in order, and the result is an intersection: a policy saying Allow may grant nothing.
- An explicit Deny anywhere ends it.
- Every applicable guardrail must allow.
- Something must grant it.
- Otherwise, nothing is permitted.

== NotAction allows everything else
section: 2. NotAction, and why it is almost always wrong
This IAM policy statement allows every AWS action except IAM's, on every resource, including services AWS launches after it was written. The NotAction element is defensible only in a Deny statement: deny everything except a narrow set.
```json
{
  "Effect": "Allow",
  "NotAction": "iam:*",
  "Resource": "*"
}
```

== ForAllValues fails open without a Null check
section: 3. Conditions, and the two set operators that fail open
Condition keys are facts AWS attaches to a request. ForAllValues is true when every value matches, and when there are none, so in an Allow it fails open. This IAM statement's Null block requires the key.
```json
{
  "Effect": "Allow",
  "Action": "ec2:DeleteTags",
  "Resource": "arn:aws:ec2:us-east-1:111122223333:instance/*",
  "Condition": {
    "ForAllValues:StringEquals": {
      "aws:TagKeys": ["environment", "cost-center"]
    },
    "Null": { "aws:TagKeys": "false" }
  }
}
```

== Test it rather than reasoning about it
section: 4. Test it rather than reasoning about it
The validate-policy command checks one policy's grammar and security warnings, reading only the text you send. This AWS CLI command asks whether new.json grants anything old.json did not, so a pipeline can fail a change that broadens access.
```bash
aws accessanalyzer check-no-new-access \
  --existing-policy-document file://old.json \
  --new-policy-document file://new.json \
  --policy-type IDENTITY_POLICY
```

== An RCP data perimeter on S3
section: 6. A data perimeter on S3
AWS Organizations runs many accounts under one management account. An RCP, written in IAM's grammar, caps what anyone can do to member accounts' resources. Part of step 6's rcp-perimeter.json, this denies S3 (Simple Storage Service) to outsiders, except AWS services.
```json
      "Effect": "Deny",
      "Principal": "*",
      "Action": "s3:*",
      "Resource": "*",
      "Condition": {
        "StringNotEqualsIfExists": { "aws:PrincipalOrgID": "o-exampleorgid" },
        "BoolIfExists": { "aws:PrincipalIsAWSService": "false" }
      }
```
credit: From AWS's data-perimeter examples (github.com/aws-samples/data-perimeter-policy-examples), cut down to S3.

== A Cedar policy set
section: 8. Install it and write a policy set
Step 8 saves policies.cedar in ~/cedar-lab, starting with this Cedar permit (MFA is multi-factor authentication). Its second policy forbids every request on a public bucket, and a forbid always beats a permit, like an explicit Deny in IAM.
```cedar
// policies.cedar
// Members of the security group may read any bucket, but only with MFA.
permit (
    principal in Group::"security",
    action == Action::"read",
    resource
)
when { context.mfa == true };
```

== A schema describes your data
section: 9. The part IAM cannot do: a schema
Step 9 saves this Cedar schema. Then cedar validate --schema store.cedarschema --policies policies.cedar passes.
```cedar
// store.cedarschema
entity Group;
entity User in [Group];
entity Bucket = {
    "public": Bool,
};

action read, write appliesTo {
    principal: [User],
    resource: [Bucket],
    context: { "mfa": Bool }
};
```

== A typo fails before the policy runs
section: 9. The part IAM cannot do: a schema
Change resource.public to resource.publik in policies.cedar, and validating again prints this. The same typo in an IAM condition key gives a valid policy that never matches, with no error at all: a Deny that silently does nothing.
```text
  × policy set validation failed
  ╰─▶ for policy `policy1`, attribute `publik` on entity type `Bucket` not
      found
    ╭─[18:8]
 17 │ )
 18 │ when { resource.publik == true };
    ·        ───────────────
    ╰────
  help: did you mean `public`?
```

== Evaluate real requests
section: 10. Evaluate real requests
Step 10 writes entities.json, where alice is in the security group and public-site is public, and MFA context files. This prints ALLOW. With ctx_nomfa.json, public-site or bob, it prints DENY. It exits non-zero on DENY, so rules can have tests.
```bash
cedar authorize --schema store.cedarschema --policies policies.cedar \
  --entities entities.json -c ctx_mfa.json \
  -l 'User::"alice"' -a 'Action::"read"' -r 'Bucket::"private-logs"'
```

== Exercise: what can this role do?
section: Hands-on exercises
A role has this IAM identity policy attached. An SCP over its account denies all actions outside eu-west-1, and the role's permissions boundary allows only s3:*. What can the role do? The guide hides each answer behind "Show the answer".
```json
{ "Effect": "Allow", "Action": "*", "Resource": "*" }
```

== Answer: every S3 action, in eu-west-1 only
section: Hands-on exercises
The identity policy grants everything, so it constrains nothing. The boundary caps the role at s3:*, and the SCP caps the account at one region. Anyone reading only the role's policy would get this completely wrong.

== Exercise: a bucket its owner may read
section: Hands-on exercises
Add a Cedar policy letting any user read a bucket they own, then extend the schema so it validates. What did the schema force you to decide that IAM would have let you leave vague?

== Answer: the schema makes you decide
section: Hands-on exercises
You had to declare that the owner attribute is a User and that every Bucket has one, so each Bucket in entities.json needs an owner. The first part is Bucket's new declaration in store.cedarschema; the second is added to policies.cedar.
```cedar
// store.cedarschema - Bucket gains an owner
entity Bucket = {
    "public": Bool,
    "owner": User,
};

// policies.cedar
permit (principal, action == Action::"read", resource)
when { resource.owner == principal };
```

== Common mistakes
section: Common mistakes
- A missing condition key usually means no match, not false.
- Wildcards are not regular expressions: only * and ? exist.
- An RCP never covers the management account's resources.
- A Cedar policy set without a schema gives up its main advantage.
